INDEX -- what is on this disk, and where it came from======================================================Every command here was tested with THIS DISK AS THE ONLY DISK (os9exec/dd/CMDS/sh), no Microware module present anywhere -- no shell, csl, cio ormath.  Note this disk does not "boot": os9exec is the kernel and simply runsa program with this disk mounted as /dd.  *  A STAR before a name means that program needs Microware's trap-handler     modules (cio, and for some csl and math).  It will NOT run on this disk     by itself: it stops with "**** Can't install trap handler ****".  Supply     your own cio and it works -- see DOC/README-CIO for how to set that up.     107 programs are starred: 94 in CMDS, 7 in CMDS/GAMES, 5 in     CMDS/REBUILT and 1 in CMDS/BROKEN.  Everything unstarred ran     standalone.  THREE PROGRAMS ARE BASIC09, not 68000 code: bio, blackjack and  wysetime.  They are I-code modules (type 2, language 2) and need  Microware's runb to interpret them, the way a starred program needs  cio.  Run them by BARE NAME with runb loaded -- `load runb` then  `bio`.  Giving runb a pathname instead raises BASIC09 error 43,  "Unknown procedure", which reads like a broken program and is not.  BASIC09's own codes are 10-80 and are not OS-9 error numbers, which  is why SYS/errmsg has no text for them.These are not broken and they are not going anywhere.  They were compiled theordinary way for the era (cc -qixm), which is a licensing question, not aquality one.Provenance: everything came from the Microware OS-9 hobbyist archive, theHamburg OS-9 Users Group, and other public preservation sites. The originalarchives are kept in CMDS/archives/ -- ed, elvis and grep include full Csource in there. Per-package documentation is in DOC/ (see DOC/README).SOURCES.txt carries fuller per-command provenance and licences.Five commands here are OUR OWN BUILDS, not archive binaries: cat, wc, basenameand dirname were rebuilt from source with the gcc2 toolchain against os9lib,because every archived build of them needs cio; and ls is our repaired buildof GNU fileutils 3.13. Their C source is on the h0 workshop disk.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- WORKS ON THIS DISK (80 commands)------------------------------------------------------  ar             archive librarian (Carl Kreider) -- .ar files  as0            68000 assembler (xasm)  as1            68010 assembler (xasm)  as11           68HC11 assembler (xasm)  as4            68040 assembler (xasm)  as5            68050 assembler (xasm)  ascii          ASCII character table  aterm          ATerm 2.6 -- terminal emulator  atob           ASCII-to-binary decode  basename       strip directory from a pathname -- REBUILT HERE with gcc2  bash           GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12 -- this disk's shell; reads .bashrc  beav           BEAV 1.40 -- Binary Editor And Viewer (needs TERM)  bison          GNU bison 1.19 parser generator -- ADDED; skeletons in /dd/LIB  btoa           Binary-to-ASCII encode  cat            concatenate files -- REBUILT HERE with gcc2 (archived cat needs cio)  cjpeg          JPEG encoder (IJG) -- ADDED  compress       compress/uncompress (LZW) -- ADDED  ctags          generate a vi tags file from C source (BSD)  date           Print date and time  dback          Directory backup utility (wants a /d0 device)  diff           GNU diff 1.1 -- ADDED; the disk had no diff at all.  Verified on CR files  dirname        strip filename from a pathname -- REBUILT HERE with gcc2  djpeg          JPEG decoder (IJG) -- ADDED  file           Identify file types (warns: no /dd/SYS/magic file -- still IDs OS-9 modules)  find           find 1.1.5 -- search a directory tree  gmake          GNU make -- ADDED (the gnu.bin build of make is the broken one)  grep           GNU grep 2.0 -- pattern search  gs33           Ghostscript 3.33 (needs gs_init.ps + fonts)  gzip           GNU gzip  hexedit      