 note                 below -- this one is a real fault, not the invocation. *wysecrack      Wyse terminal baud detect -- needs real Wyse hardwarebio was here too, reported as "exits with error 43".  It is not broken andhas moved to /dd/CMDS.  Error 43 is BASIC09's "Unknown procedure", and onedocumented cause is invoking runb with a PATH: runb takes a bare modulename.  Loaded and called by name it draws its chart correctly.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC139 and /dd/CMDS/GCC2 -- the gcc toolchains (15)------------------------------------------------------Two generations of GNU C for OS-9/68k, kept apart from /dd/CMDS because thepass programs (cc1, cccp, collect) are driven by the compiler, not by you.Microware's `cc` is the compiler for almost everything on this disk; gcc ishere because a few programs need ANSI C, which Microware's K&R cc cannotparse.  `ls` is the one on this disk that was built this way.  GCC139 (7) gcc gcc_cc1 gcc_cc1plus gcc_cccp gcc_collect gpp makeinfo  GCC2   (8) cc1plus cc2 cc2plus cccp2 collect gcc gcc2 gpp------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/REBUILT -- alternates, NOT installed over the originals------------------------------------------------------  Alternates for names this disk already uses. The curated binary  is still the one in /dd/CMDS; these are here so you can compare  or swap them yourself.  Most are our own source builds. Two are not: arc and VI came from  the old NONFREE directory, where they had been recorded as SDK  binaries in error. Both are third-party programs that happen to  arrive under a name already taken -- VI is a PVIC build, a  sibling of vi_nocio.  arc            ARC v5.12, third-party; /dd/CMDS/arc is a later build  VI             PVIC, public domain Portable VI Clone *basename.cio   archived build, needs cio; superseded by the gcc2 rebuild *cat.cio        archived build, needs cio; superseded by the gcc2 rebuild  